now this may not be a general election  but these polls and the results could  prove incredibly important  across much of britain but before we get  to that let's take a moment to consider  what's going on and the short answer is  quite a lot actually there are depending  on how you count it actually seven  or eight separate things happening here  so first of all  you've got the scottish and welsh  parliament elections they happen every  five years there's the english council  elections the local elections in this  case there's actually two years worth of  them bundled into one because last  year's cancelled  due to covert you've got the london  assembly elections and the vote for the  london  mayor those were also postponed and  you've got another 12 elected mayors  across various metropolitan and  regional areas of england you've got  police and crime commissioner elections  too and as if  that weren't all enough you've also got  a parliamentary by-election to elect a  new mp  for hartlepool to make it more  complicated i'm afraid  many of these different elections  involve slightly different voting  systems so  hartlepool that's simple as with general  elections the candidate with the most  votes  wins but the scottish and welsh  elections they involve something called  the additional  member system you vote once for a  candidate in your local constituency and  a second time  for a party those second votes go  towards a regional list  of members of these parliaments it's a  form of proportional representation the  idea being  to ensure that smaller parties get  represented in the parliaments and it  makes getting a majority very difficult  indeed though  not impossible the additional member  system that's also used for the london  assembly election but  not for the london mayor that election  uses a different voting system the  supplementary vote where you choose  a first and a second choice candidate  it's not proportional it's not a  proportional representation  but it's designed to find a candidate  who most people either want or  kind of want same system supplementary  vote for the directly elected mayors  elsewhere around the uk and  the police and crime commissioners too  you get the idea  seven or eight elections three different  voting systems three days maybe more  of counting as we start to piece  together the latest picture  of politics around great britain
 

